# Astronomical Poetry Dating

The silver moon is set;
The Pleiades are gone;
Half the long night is spent, and yet
I lie alone.

This is the John Herman Merivale translation of fragment "168B Voigt" by Sappho,
often known as the Midnight Poem. In 2016, scholars combined the information 
in the poem, the location of the poet, and astronomical calculations to provide
an estimate of when the poem was written (Cuntz et al, 2016). That particular
estimate was 25 January - 6 April using the year 570 BC. This project 
reproduces the calculation using current data science tools. It also explores 
how sensitive the result is to parameter selection, the details of which are 
the result of deep scholarship. Indeed, scholars point out the poem may not 
even be by Sappho.  (See ##Authorship Debate.)

The calculation assumes the report of seeing the constellation at a
particular time is factual, rather than fictional. It also places Sappho
at Mytilene. The poem's other astronomical detail, the setting Moon, is
not used as a constraint here either, following the paper. (See
##Moon Phase.)

Our analysis reproduces the 2016 paper, but also shows the result is sensitive
to varying parameters which are necessarily estimates. We reproduce the
calculated date of 25 January 570 BC, but only when refraction adjustment
is turned off, unlike the paper. With refraction adjustment, we get the
26th.

We also perform a calculation adjusting for the height of the west
horizon at Mytilene. This gives a date of 23 January, which varies from
the paper, which comments that horizon adjustment does not change the
result.

Finally we check how sensitive the calculation is to varying estimated, ancient
locations and dates within a range, in ##Plausible Range. Using ranges, we 
find that an estimated January-February season is quite stable throughout 
Sappho's adult life. This is a narrower time range than the January-April range
in the paper. A range is a more useful guide than a single date considering
the basis for some of these parameters, e.g., midnight in a technological era
without precise clocks. We test a range of values around the time of night and 
the elevation of Mytilene. Whether it was Sappho or another poet, and whether 
she was sixteen or sixty, we can imagine her in the sharp Mediterranean winter 
air, looking at the stars, alone, remembering.



##Astronomical Calculation Overview

    def calculate_date(location,time_range,astro_ref,horizon_adjust=0,
                        refraction=True,midnight_offset_hours=0.0) \
            -> HistoricalDate :
        reference = time_range[1]
        star = _star(astro_ref)
        setting_altitude = 3.0 + horizon_adjust
        start = HistoricalDate(reference.year, 1, 1, reference.era)
        for candidate in _dates_from(start, 120):
            time = _local_midnight(candidate, location, midnight_offset_hours)
            altitude = _altitude_degrees(star, location, time, refraction)
            if altitude <= setting_altitude:
                return candidate
        return None

`calculate_date` follows the paper's search: starting from New Year's Day of
the reference year, walk forward day by day and return the first date on
which the reference star has already set — altitude at or below 3 degrees,
raised by any ##Horizon Adjustment — by local midnight.

The paper's own published figure comes from a calculation that includes
atmospheric refraction (see ##Refraction), which is the
default here too. But the result is sensitive enough to sub-degree
refraction modelling that our refraction-inclusive figure lands a day
later than theirs; disabling refraction happens to reproduce their
published date exactly, which we take as a coincidence of where the
threshold falls relative to a day boundary, not as evidence refraction
should be off.

~example
    calculate_date(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE
    ) == "26 January 570 BC"

This is the result from the 2016 paper — reproduced by turning refraction
off, which is not what the paper itself did.

~example
    calculate_date(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE, refraction=False
    ) == "25 January 570 BC"


Sappho lived from 630 to 570 BC; the poem is dated within her adult life,
from age sixteen to her death.

    SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE = (
        HistoricalDate(614, 1, 1, "BC"), HistoricalDate(570, 1, 1, "BC"))

The location of Mytilene, and the Hipparcos catalog number for Alcyone.

    MYTILENE =  (39.11, 26.55)
    PLEIADES_ALCYONE = 17702



## Calculation Details

The astronomical calculation uses skyfield to interpolate. We choose de406,
a JPL ephemeris covering 3000 BC - 3000 AD, and the Hipparcos catalog for
star positions, since precession over two and a half millennia is too large
to ignore.

Local time on Lesbos is taken as a fixed offset from UT based on Mytilene's
longitude, following the paper's own convention rather than a modern time
zone. Dates are read against the proleptic Julian calendar, the calendar in
use at the time.

    EPHEMERIS_DIRECTORY = "var/ephemeris"
    JULIAN_MONTH_LENGTHS = [31,28,31,30,31,30,31,31,30,31,30,31]

The ephemeris, timescale, and star catalog are each expensive to load, so
each is fetched at most once and cached for the life of the process.

    @lru_cache(maxsize=1)
    def _loader():
        return Loader(EPHEMERIS_DIRECTORY)

    @lru_cache(maxsize=1)
    def _earth():
        return _loader()('de406.bsp')['earth']

    @lru_cache(maxsize=1)
    def _timescale():
        return _loader().timescale()

    @lru_cache(maxsize=1)
    def _hipparcos_catalog():
        with _loader().open(hipparcos.URL) as f:
            return hipparcos.load_dataframe(f)

    @cache
    def _star(hip_number):
        return Star.from_dataframe(_hipparcos_catalog().loc[hip_number])

The search in `calculate_date` walks the reference year day by day, so it
needs to step a `HistoricalDate` forward and convert each one to a Julian
day number. Both need the proleptic Julian calendar's leap rule, since the
Gregorian reform is over two thousand years in the future at this date.

    def _is_julian_leap_year(astronomical_year):
        return astronomical_year % 4 == 0

    def _days_in_month(astronomical_year, month):
        if month == 2 and _is_julian_leap_year(astronomical_year):
            return 29
        return JULIAN_MONTH_LENGTHS[month - 1]

    def _dates_from(start, count):
        year, month, day, era = start.year, start.month, start.day, start.era
        for _ in range(count):
            yield HistoricalDate(year, month, day, era)
            day += 1
            if day > _days_in_month(start.astronomical_year, month):
                day, month = 1, month + 1

    def _julian_day(historical_date, hour=0.0):
        year, month, day = historical_date.astronomical_year, \
            historical_date.month, historical_date.day
        if month <= 2:
            year, month = year - 1, month + 12
        return (int(365.25 * (year + 4716)) + int(30.6001 * (month + 1))
                + day + hour / 24 - 1524.5)

"Midnight on date D" names the night of D, which ends at local 00:00 the
following calendar day — not the midnight that opens D — so the search
tests the *following* day's Julian-day-zero instant for each candidate.
That instant itself is only as precise as the water clock the paper
assumes the poem's midnight was read from (see ##Plausible Range), so an
`hour_offset` lets that clock be run fast or slow.

    def _local_midnight(historical_date, location, hour_offset=0.0):
        _, longitude = location
        offset_hours = longitude / 15.0
        return _timescale().ut1_jd(
            _julian_day(historical_date) + 1
            - offset_hours / 24.0 + hour_offset / 24.0)


##Refraction

The paper's own numeric result includes atmospheric refraction — its note
8 attributes a mismatch between a screenshot and a computed number to
exactly this — so it is on here by default, and can be switched off to
compare against an unrefracted altitude.

    def _altitude_degrees(star, location, time, refraction=True):
        latitude, longitude = location
        observer = _earth() + wgs84.latlon(latitude, longitude)
        apparent = observer.at(time).observe(star).apparent()
        if refraction:
            altitude, _, _ = apparent.altaz(
                temperature_C="standard", pressure_mbar="standard")
        else:
            altitude, _, _ = apparent.altaz()
        return altitude.degrees


## Horizon Adjustment

The 2016 paper does not take into account that stars set in the west, and
Mt Olympus of Lesbos is to the west of Mytilene. So the horizon is higher,
and the Pleiades would set earlier. `horizon_adjust` returns the angle by
which the effective horizon is raised, seen from `from_location` towards
the peak at `to_location`, net of the observer's own elevation, given the
peak's height and great-circle distance.

    EARTH_RADIUS_METRES = 6371000.0

    def _great_circle_metres(from_location, to_location):
        lat1, lon1 = radians(from_location[0]), radians(from_location[1])
        lat2, lon2 = radians(to_location[0]), radians(to_location[1])
        d_lat, d_lon = lat2 - lat1, lon2 - lon1
        h = sin(d_lat / 2) ** 2 + cos(lat1) * cos(lat2) * sin(d_lon / 2) ** 2
        return 2 * EARTH_RADIUS_METRES * asin(sqrt(h))

    def horizon_adjust(from_location,to_location,from_elevation=0) -> float:
        to_latitude, to_longitude, to_elevation = to_location
        distance = _great_circle_metres(
            from_location, (to_latitude, to_longitude))
        return degrees(atan2(to_elevation - from_elevation, distance))


Mt Olympus of Lesbos is the island's highest peak, given here as latitude,
longitude, and its height in metres.

    MT_OLYMPUS_OF_LESBOS = (39.28, 26.33, 968)

We do not know the exact date of the paper's horizon-adjusted calculation.
The paper suggests it does not change the outcome, but our model of the
horizon disagrees. See ##Astronomical Calculation Overview for just how
sensitive this search is to sub-degree changes in the threshold.

~example
    calculate_date(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE,
        horizon_adjust(MYTILENE, MT_OLYMPUS_OF_LESBOS)
    ) == "23 January 570 BC"

~example
    calculate_date(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE,
        horizon_adjust(MYTILENE, MT_OLYMPUS_OF_LESBOS), refraction=False
    ) == "23 January 570 BC"

With the horizon raised, refraction no longer changes the day: both
variants land on 23 January, unlike the flat-horizon case above.


## Plausible Range

`horizon_adjust` above assumed Mytilene itself sits at sea level, but the
paper places it on the range's lower eastern slopes, "at an altitude
between 200m and 900m." Rather than pick one figure, `calculate_date_range`
sweeps both ends and reports the earliest and latest result — a lower
Mytilene means a bigger net dip to the peak, hence an earlier date; a
higher Mytilene means a smaller dip and a later one. Refraction and the
horizon adjustment stay on throughout.

    MYTILENE_ELEVATION_RANGE = (200, 900)

    def calculate_date_range(location, time_range, astro_ref, peak,
                              elevation_range):
        return tuple(
            calculate_date(
                location, time_range, astro_ref,
                horizon_adjust(location, peak, from_elevation=elevation))
            for elevation in elevation_range)

~example
    calculate_date_range(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE,
        MT_OLYMPUS_OF_LESBOS, MYTILENE_ELEVATION_RANGE
    ) == ("23 January 570 BC", "25 January 570 BC")

Even holding refraction and the horizon model fixed, the single "true"
date the paper reports turns out to be one point in a small range driven
by an input the paper itself only bounds loosely, not a solved constant.

"Midnight" is a second such input, and a shakier one: the paper admits it
could not establish what kind of time-keeping device was used on Lesbos
around 570 BC, only assuming a clepsydra. An outflow water clock is not a
precise instrument, so treating its "midnight" as exact to the minute
assumes more precision than the source can support. Unlike the elevation
bound above, the paper gives no figure for this, so a two-hour window —
plus or minus an hour — here is our own placeholder judgement call, not
a sourced number.

    MIDNIGHT_UNCERTAINTY_HOURS = 1.0
    MIDNIGHT_OFFSET_RANGE = (-MIDNIGHT_UNCERTAINTY_HOURS,
                              MIDNIGHT_UNCERTAINTY_HOURS)

    def calculate_date_midnight_range(location, time_range, astro_ref,
                                       offset_range):
        return tuple(
            calculate_date(location, time_range, astro_ref,
                           midnight_offset_hours=offset)
            for offset in offset_range)

~example
    calculate_date_midnight_range(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE, MIDNIGHT_OFFSET_RANGE
    ) == ("10 February 570 BC", "10 January 570 BC")

Alcyone's local setting time drifts only a few minutes earlier each night
— the ordinary sidereal/solar mismatch — so the search's date-per-night
resolution is coarse: a plausible hour of clock error either way is
enough to walk the result across an entire calendar month. That dwarfs
the elevation range above, and it means the day-level precision the
paper reports rests on assuming a water clock far more exact than the
paper's own note about it can support.

The paper's own note 7 makes a different, checkable claim: that which
year of Sappho's adult life you assume barely matters, since precession
moves Alcyone too slowly over a few decades to shift the result. Unlike
elevation and midnight precision, this isn't a source of fragility we are
introducing — it's the paper's own robustness claim, tested here by
running `calculate_date` at both ends of #SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, 614 and
570 BC, forty-four years apart, holding everything else fixed.

    def calculate_date_year_range(location, time_range, astro_ref):
        start_year, end_year = time_range
        return (
            calculate_date(location, (start_year, start_year), astro_ref),
            calculate_date(location, (end_year, end_year), astro_ref),
        )

~example
    calculate_date_year_range(
        MYTILENE, SAPPHO_ADULT_LIFE, PLEIADES_ALCYONE
    ) == ("25 January 614 BC", "26 January 570 BC")

Forty-four years apart, and the day of year moves by one. The paper's
insensitivity claim holds up here — a single day across her entire adult
life is indeed "no appreciable difference" — in sharp contrast to
elevation and midnight precision, which move the result by days and
weeks respectively for inputs the paper barely constrains at all.



---

#Appendix

## Moon Phase

The poem also has the Moon set, but — as the paper notes — this cannot be
used to further constrain the date, and `calculate_date` does not model
the Moon at all. A true new moon is excluded by construction even so: the
Moon trails the Sun eastward by about 12.2° a day, roughly 49 minutes of
extra moonset lag per day of lunar age. At new moon that lag is near zero,
so the Moon would set with the Sun — but the poem has it setting with
"half the long night... spent," which needs several hours of lag, and so
several days of lunar age. Herschberg and Mebius's (1990) independent
estimate of a 2–6 day crescent is consistent with this reading, though it
is an argument from the poem's wording rather than a computed constraint.


## Authorship Debate

This fragment is attested in antique sources but only attributed to Sappho
during the Renaissance by Arsenius Apostolius.


## Greek Text of the Poem

Δέδυκε μὲν ἀ σελάννα
καὶ Πληΐαδες, μέσαι δέ
νύκτες, πάρα δ' ἔρχετ' ὤρα,
ἔγω δὲ μόνα κατεύδω.


## Poem Identification

There are multiple naming schemes for fragmentary ancient texts. The best 
accepted scheme for this poem is "fr. 168B Voigt (1971)", named after a well 
known edition of the poems. The Cuntz et al (2016) paper uses Bergk's naming, 
which is fragment 52, the same as in Wharton's translation.


## Development Process Notes

This project was started with a hand authored high level specification and 
design stub in dating.lob. This comprised a text description, two function 
signatures with empty implementations for calculate_date() and 
horizon_adjust(), and two ~example tests. The code compiled and linted but the 
unit tests failed, in a consciously TDD style.

A coding agent (Claude Code with Sonnet 5) was then introduced to implement the
project. The project grew with mostly arms-length dialogue around the
implementation, inspecting the impact of varying parameters was added through
prompting after the first running version. Most changes were performed by the
coding agent, though there were also manual edits for structure, style and
clarity. The project took about a day to implement, multi-tasking with other
work.


