## Test Fixtures

The tests appendix below only holds assertions, not definitions, so these
synthetic points — not the real Mytilene/Lesbos figures, chosen so the
expected angles come out exact — live here instead.

    HERE = (0.0, 0.0)
    NEAR = (0.0, 1.0, 1000.0)
    FAR = (0.0, 2.0, 1000.0)



#Tests

Geometry checks for `horizon_adjust`, using the synthetic points defined
in ##Test Fixtures.

##a peak directly overhead raises the horizon to the zenith
    horizon_adjust(HERE, (0.0, 0.0, 1000.0)) == 90.0

##a peak level with the observer raises the horizon not at all
    horizon_adjust(HERE, (0.0, 1.0, 0.0)) == 0.0

##a closer peak of the same height raises the horizon more steeply
    horizon_adjust(HERE, NEAR) > horizon_adjust(HERE, FAR)

##doubling the distance approximately halves the angle, at this scale
    abs(horizon_adjust(HERE, NEAR) - 2 * horizon_adjust(HERE, FAR)) < 0.01

##one degree of latitude matches the standard 111.2 km approximation
    abs(_great_circle_metres(HERE, (1.0, 0.0)) - 111194.9) < 1.0


